---
import Container from "@components/Container.astro";
import BackToPrev from "@components/BackToPrev.astro";
import PageLayout from "@layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Link from "@components/Link.astro";
import SectionHeading from "@components/SectionHeading.astro";
import ContactBlock from "@components/ContactBlock.astro";

import { NOW } from "@consts";

// Bump this when the page content changes
const updated = new Date("2026-10-12");
---

<PageLayout title={NOW.TITLE} description={NOW.DESCRIPTION}>
  <Container>
    <div class="animate mb-8">
      <BackToPrev href="/">
        Back to home
      </BackToPrev>
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-14">
      <!-- Header -->
      <section class="animate space-y-5 border-b border-black/10 pb-8 dark:border-white/10">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2">
          <span class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-black/65 dark:text-white/65">
            Now // Updated {updated.toLocaleDateString("en-GB", { day: "numeric", month: "short", year: "numeric" })}
          </span>
          <a
            href="https://nownownow.com/about"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="font-mono text-[11px] text-black/65 hover:text-black dark:text-white/65 dark:hover:text-white transition"
          >
            What's a now page? ↗
          </a>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-black dark:text-white">
          What keeps me busy <span class="font-serif italic font-normal text-black/65 dark:text-white/65">at the moment.</span>
        </h1>
        <article class="space-y-4 text-black/75 dark:text-white/75">
          <p>
            Late summer into autumn has a way of resetting the slate. The days are shifting, and after the daily creative grind, I actually want to make things that last. The to-do list, though? That bastard only grows. From design systems that live half-finished in my second brain to house repairs, garden experiments, and the endless pottering that makes a place feel like home—it’s a juggling act. One part therapy, one part chaos.
          </p>
          <p>
            I’m reading more, stepping away from notification loops, and taking my time. Life’s good.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- Day to Day: Work & Focus -->
      <section class="animate space-y-4">
        <SectionHeading title="01. The Search: Finding the Next Role" />
        <article class="space-y-3 text-black/75 dark:text-white/75">
          <p>
            Day to day, my primary focus is finding my next design role. I’m taking a deliberate, grounded approach rather than firing off CVs into the void.
          </p>
          <p>
            I’m looking for a calm, intentional team—ideally remote, though the right in-house team is just as welcome. What matters most to me is environment: low-friction cultures that value deep focus, respect asynchronous communication, and know that quality design comes from thoughtful problem-solving rather than frantic, back-to-back meetings.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- Building small tools -->
      <section class="animate space-y-4">
        <SectionHeading title="02. Building Small Tools" />
        <article class="space-y-3 text-black/75 dark:text-white/75">
          <p>
            Between applications, I've been building the small apps I wish already existed. Each one removes a step I was tired of repeating, and each one has taught me a new toolchain along the way. I wrote about the thinking behind the first two in <Link href="/blog/11-tiny-tools-less-friction/">Tiny Tools, Less Friction</Link>.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-black dark:text-white"><Link href="/projects/auto-folder-creator/">Auto Folder Creator</Link>:</strong> a Windows desktop app that builds a project's folders, brief and job number in one keystroke. It's the grown-up version of my old Caps Lock + D script.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-black dark:text-white"><Link href="/projects/obsidian-capture/">Vault Capture</Link>:</strong> an Android app that drops tasks and links straight into my Obsidian vault, including from the Share sheet.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-black dark:text-white"><Link href="/projects/folder-tidy/">Folder Tidy</Link>:</strong> the newest one. A Windows app that clears out empty folders, sorts finished downloads into the right place and lets me undo any of it. It's built and in daily use, and I'm still polishing it before it goes public.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Next up is getting the code tidy enough to publish, and working out which annoyance gets an app next.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- Digital & Physical Mise-en-Place -->
      <section class="animate space-y-4">
        <SectionHeading title="03. Digital Mise-en-Place & Guardrails" />
        <article class="space-y-3 text-black/75 dark:text-white/75">
          <p>
            When focus is finite, small paper cuts in your setup drain your bandwidth before you even touch real work. Lately, I’ve been applying strict rules to protect my working memory and attention:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-black dark:text-white">Decluttering the Digital Field:</strong> Stripping out unused apps, dead bookmarks, and 40-tag folder trees that inevitably fail. Moving toward flat directories, a single drop inbox, and fast search so my digital desk is as clean as my physical workspace.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-black dark:text-white">Batching Over Infinite Scrolling:</strong> Putting strict time boundaries on job boards and feeds to kill search fatigue and protect hyperfocus for actual design problems.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-black dark:text-white">Analog Buffer First:</strong> Catching ideas and scratchpad logic in a pocket dot-grid notebook before reaching for a screen. Paper carries zero notification traps.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-black dark:text-white">The Sensory Shield:</strong> Using active noise cancellation and familiar soundscapes to mute ambient household and street static whenever I need deep focus.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- Server & Hardware Tinkering -->
      <section class="animate space-y-4">
        <SectionHeading title="04. Hardware & Server Overhaul" />
        <article class="space-y-3 text-black/75 dark:text-white/75">
          <p>
            <strong class="text-black dark:text-white">Moving Away from Hypervisor Bloat:</strong> I’m planning a move away from Proxmox. While it served its purpose, managing complex virtualization and nested containers stopped being fun and turned into an administrative chore. I’m currently planning a simple, bare-metal server build to keep running costs down and maintenance minimal—tools should serve you, not demand weekend maintenance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-black dark:text-white">3D Printing Pause:</strong> 3D printing has come to a temporary halt. I’ve outgrown my current setup and am weighing up whether to invest in a more reliable, low-maintenance printer later down the line.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-black dark:text-white">The Air-Gapped Keychain Station:</strong> Completed my emergency USB-C rescue drive. Half is partitioned for Ventoy live environments, and the rest hosts an air-gapped terminal dashboard with local offline documentation so I’m never stranded by connectivity hiccups.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- Reading -->
      <section class="animate space-y-4">
        <SectionHeading title="05. What I'm Reading" />
        <article class="space-y-3 text-black/75 dark:text-white/75">
          <p>
            <strong class="text-black dark:text-white">Project Hail Mary by Andy Weir</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Diving into <em>Project Hail Mary</em>. It’s hard science fiction at its most engaging: a lone astronaut waking up with amnesia on a desperate, one-way interstellar mission, forced to science his way out of catastrophic problems one controlled experiment at a time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weir nails that methodical, iterative problem-solving loop where panic is set aside in favour of testing hypotheses, building bespoke tools out of whatever is at hand, and documenting findings. It’s practical, brilliant, and completely engrossing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <Link href="https://www.amazon.co.uk/Project-Hail-Mary-Andy-Weir/dp/1529157463" external>
              More info on Amazon UK &rarr;
            </Link>
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <ContactBlock />
    </div>
  </Container>
</PageLayout>